            <ArticleTitle>Argus T® versus Advance® Sling for postprostatectomy urinary incontinence: A randomized clinical trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>531-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/S1677-5538.IBJU.2015.0075</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the results of two slings, Argus T® and Advance®, for the treatment of postprostatectomy urinary incontinence (PPUI).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From December 2010 to December 2011, 22 patients with PPUI were randomized as follows: 11 (mean age 62.09(±5.30)) underwent treatment with Advance® and 11 (mean age 62.55(±8.54)) with Argus T®. All patients were evaluated preoperatively with urodynamic testing, quality of life questionnaire (ICIQ-SF), voiding diary and 24-hour pad test. Exclusion criteria were: neurological diseases, severe detrusor overactivity and urethral stenosis. Evaluation was performed at 6, 12 and 18 months after the surgery. After implantation of the Argus T® sling, patients who experienced urine leakage equal to or greater than the initial volume underwent adjustment of the sling tension. Results were statistically analyzed using the Fisher's test, Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, Friedman's non-parametric test or the Mann-Whitney test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Significant improvement of the 24-hour pad test was observed with the Argus T® sling (p=0.038), With regard to the other parameters, there was no significant difference between the two groups. Removal of the Argus T® device due to perineal pain was performed in one patient (9%). Despite non uniform results, both devices were considered useful to improve quality of life (ICIQ-SF): Argus T® (p=0.018) and Advance® (p=0.017).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Better results were observed in the 24h pad test and in levels of satisfactionwith the Argus T® device. Both slings contributed to improve quality of life (ICIQ-SF), with acceptable side effects.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
